{%- comment -%}
  Bassface accordion row — one collapsible section of the product page's stack.

  Accepts:
    label   - the row heading. Required.
    content - the panel's HTML. Optional; the row renders nothing at all when
              this is blank, so an empty product description leaves no header
              opening onto nothing.
    id      - a unique fragment for the element ids. Required when more than
              one row is on the page, which is always.

  Usage:
    {%- capture body %}{{ product.description }}{% endcapture -%}
    {%- render 'bf-accordion', label: 'Product Description', content: body, id: 'desc' -%}

  The consumer loads bf-accordion.css.

  <details>/<summary>, not a button and a div. Open and close, keyboard
  operation, the disclosure semantics and find-in-page expanding a closed
  section are all native — the JS version of this reimplements four things the
  element already does. There is no bf-accordion.js for the same reason: the
  plus becoming a minus is a `details[open]` rule in CSS.

  Every row starts closed. That is the whole of the decision — there is no open
  state to configure, so nothing here takes an `open` argument.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if content != blank -%}
  <bf-accordion>
    <details class="accordion" id="Accordion-{{ id }}">
      <summary class="accordion__header">
        <span class="accordion__label">{{ label }}</span>

        {%- comment -%}
          The plus is DRAWN, not an icon — two pseudo-element bars on one span.
          It has to morph into the minus rather than be swapped for it: the
          whole mark rotates a quarter turn while the bar that would end up
          vertical collapses, so the two strokes resolve into one. Two <svg>
          glyphs cross-fading cannot do that, and a rotating plus is
          indistinguishable from a still one, so both halves are needed.

          No element inside it, so nothing here is an empty box needing filler.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="accordion__toggle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </summary>

      {%- comment -%}
        Two boxes, not one. The outer is the clipper whose height is animated;
        the padding lives on the inner. With padding on the clipper, `height: 0`
        still leaves the 48px of it standing, so the last of the panel vanished
        in one frame at the end of every close instead of closing with it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="accordion__panel">
        <div class="accordion__body">{{ content }}</div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </bf-accordion>
{%- endif -%}
